.TH REALLOC 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
realloc \- resize a block of memory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.BI "void* realloc(void* " address ", size_t " byte_count ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR realloc ()
resizes a block returned by Crunch
.BR malloc (3crunch)
or
.BR realloc ().
The
.I byte_count
specifies the new size in bytes.
The allocation may move. On success, use the returned pointer instead of
the old one.
The contents are preserved up to the smaller of the old and new requested
sizes; newly added bytes have unspecified contents.
.PP
If
.I address
is null, the call behaves like
.BR malloc (3crunch),
including for a zero size.
If
.I address
is nonnull and
.I byte_count
is zero, the block is released and null is returned.
After that call, treat the original pointer as invalid even if releasing the
underlying mapping reports an error.
.SH RETURN VALUE
For a nonzero size, success returns a nonnull, 16\-byte\-aligned pointer and
leaves
.B errno
unchanged.
Failure returns null, sets
.BR errno ,
and leaves the original allocation valid and unchanged.
This includes failure to release the old arena during a moving resize.
.PP
For a nonnull pointer and zero size, null indicates release rather than
allocation failure. An underlying unmap failure sets
.BR errno ;
otherwise
.B errno
is unchanged.
Set
.B errno
to zero before the call if that distinction matters.
.SH ERRORS
Include
.B <errno.h>
to inspect
.BR errno .
.TP
.B ENOMEM
The size is too large for the allocator, or the requested
memory or mapping resize could not be obtained.
.PP
Other Linux mapping or unmapping errors can also be reported.
.SH EXAMPLE
Use a temporary pointer so a failed resize does not lose the original:
.PP
.nf
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    char* data = malloc(16);
    if (!data) return 1;
    data[0] = (char)42;
    char* grown = realloc(data, 4096);
    if (!grown) {
        free(data);
        return 2;
    }
    data = grown;
    int result = data[0] == 42 ? 0 : 3;
    free(data);
    return result;
}
.fi
.SH NOTES
Only current pointers from this allocator may be passed to
.BR realloc ().
Do not mix them with pointers managed by the system C allocator or
.BR munmap .
The allocator is not thread\-safe; Crunch
.B errno
is process\-wide.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR malloc (3crunch),
.BR free (3crunch),
.BR allocator_trim (3crunch),
.BR crunch (1)
